.TH LIBMPQ__PATCH_ADD_PATH 3 2026-11-03 "The MoPaQ archive library"
.SH NAME
libmpq__patch_add_path \- stage an ordinary new member from a filesystem path
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <libmpq/mpq.h>
.sp
.BI "int32_t libmpq__patch_add_path("
.BI "        mpq_patch_s *" "patch,"
.BI "        const char *" "filename,"
.BI "        const char *" "source_path,"
.BI "        const mpq_file_options_s *" "options"
.BI ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Stage an ordinary physical member whose nonempty filename is absent from the
base archive and all staged identities. This is an addition, not a PTCH
replacement or delete marker. No base file is modified. Addition-only patches
need no metadata marker when explicitly supplied to
\fBlibmpq__archive_open_patch\fP().
.PP
NULL \fIoptions\fP selects ordinary writer defaults. Existing compression,
encryption, FIX_KEY, single-unit, sector CRC and representable locale/platform
options are supported. PATCH_FILE, DELETE_MARKER and unsupported flags are
rejected. The common finalizer generates v4 structural and raw-chunk MD5 over
final stored bytes, using the existing public 16 KiB raw-chunk default.
.PP
Read \fIsource_path\fP in bounded chunks during the call. The source file
is not modified or retained, and filesystem timestamps are not imported.
.PP
Existing base or staged identities are rejected. Ambiguous hashes or
locale/platform identities are rejected without guessing filenames. Removing
a base member does not make it eligible for addition; use replacement.
A staged addition cannot subsequently be replaced or removed on this handle.
.PP
Arguments and options are validated before output where possible; such errors
preserve earlier valid stages. Partial output failures latch a terminal error
and prevent successful publication. Finish and abort consume the patch handle,
including on error. Abort discards unpublished output. The existing MPQE
staging/publication and signing restrictions are unchanged.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Return 0 on success or an existing negative libmpq error code on failure.
.SH ERRORS
Missing required pointers, empty filenames, and existing identities return
LIBMPQ_ERROR_EXIST. Ambiguous identities, embedded line breaks in names,
reserved metadata names, and unsupported options return LIBMPQ_ERROR_FORMAT.
Invalid sizes return LIBMPQ_ERROR_SIZE. Allocation, filesystem, compression
and encryption errors propagate using the existing error codes.
.SH SEE ALSO
